What is the difference between isotopologues and isotopomers?,"<bos><start_of_turn>user
What is the difference between isotopologues and isotopomers?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The terms ""isotopologues"" and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. The key difference lies in the number of isotopic substitutions:  
1. **Isotopoplogues**: These are molecules that differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) (cyclohexane) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_6 \) (hexaduterated cyclohexane) are isotopologues because they differ only in the replacement of hydrogen atoms with deuterium.  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that differ in the isotopic composition of all atoms, but have the same molecular formula. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_6 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{D}_6 \) are isotopomers because they have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of all atoms.  

Thus, **isotopomers are a subset of isotopologues**, where all atoms are replaced with isotopes.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isopologues: Different isotopic composition of one or more atoms.} \\
&\text{Isotomers: Different isotopic composition of all atoms, same molecular formula.}
\end{aligned}
}
