In molecular orbital theory, what is the key difference between a σ molecular orbital and a π molecular orbital in terms of electron density distribution?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The key difference between σ molecular orbitals (σ) and π molecular orbitals (π) lies in the electron density distribution:  
- **σ molecular orbitals** have electron density concentrated along the internuclear axis (the line between the nuclei of the bonded atoms).  
- **π molecular orbitals** have electron density concentrated above and below the internuclear axis, perpendicular to the internuclear axis.  

Thus, σ orbitals are formed by head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, while π orbitals are formed by lateral overlap of atomic orbitals.  

$\boxed{\text{σ orbital: electron density along the internuclear axis}}$  
$\boxed{\text{π orbital: electron density above/below the internuclear axis}}$
