","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when molecules have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their structures. In the case of C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism: chain isomerism and functional group isomerism.

   a) Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon skeleton of the molecule can be arranged in different ways. For example, butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and 2-methylpropan-1-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH3) are chain isomers.

   b) Functional group isomerism: This occurs when the molecule contains different functional groups. For example, C4H10O can be either an alcohol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) or an ether (CH3CH2OCH3).

