","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis where the reaction produces a product with a single enantiomer (a single stereoisomer) with a 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means the reaction produces only one stereoisomer of the product, with no of the other enantiomer present. This is the highest level of enantioselectivity possible in asymmetric synthesis.

The other types of asymmetric synthesis do not achieve this level of selectivity. For example, in a reaction with high enantioselectivity, the reaction may produce 90% of one enantiomer and 10% of the other. This is still useful for producing enantiomerically enriched products, but it does not be considered ""absolute"" asymmetric synthesis.

In contrast, in absolute asymmetric synthesis, the absolute configuration of the product is predetermined by the reaction conditions, typically by using a chiral catalyst or reagent. This means that the reaction will always produce the same enantiomer of the product, regardless of the starting material or reaction conditions.

An example of absolute asymmetric synthesis is the hydrogenation of a prochiral olefin using a chiral rhodium complex as a catalyst. In this reaction, the chiral rhodium complex selectively hydrogenates one face of the olefin, leading to the formation of one enantiomer of the chiral alcohol product. The absolute configuration of the product can be determined by the structure of the chiral rhodium complex used as the catalyst.

In summary, absolute asymmetric synthesis is the most efficient and selective way to produce enantiomerically pure compounds. It is achieved by using chiral catalysts or reagents that selectively control the stereochemistry of the reaction, resulting in the formation of one enantiomer of the product with 100% ee.
