"Write an equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Write an equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The equation of the tangent line to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by:

\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]

**Explanation:**
- \( f'(a) \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( x = a \), which represents the slope of the tangent line at \( (a, f(a)) \).
- The slope-intercept form of a line is \( y = mx + b \), where \( m \) is the slope and \( b \) is the \( y \)-intercept.
- Substituting \( f'(a) \) for \( m \) and \( f(a) \) for \( b \) gives the equation of the tangent line.

Thus, the equation \( y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a) \) is the equation of the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at \( (a, f(a)) \).
