","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in the best-case case of Completely-Sorted Input is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case**: If \( n = 1 \), the input is a single element, so no comparisons are needed. Thus, \( C(1) = 0 \).
2. **Main Case**: For \( n > 1 \), the pivot element splits the input into two equal-sized subleaves of size \( \lfloor n/2 \rfloor \) and \( \lceil n/2 \rfloor \).
   - The first term \( C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor \) represents the number of comparisons in the first sublevel.
   - The second term \( C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil \) represents the number of comparisons in the second sublevel.
   - The subtraction of 1 accounts for the single comparison to compare the pivot element with the first element of the second sublevel, which determines the placement of the pivot.

This recurrence is derived under the assumption that the pivot element splits the sublist into two equal-length leaves, which is the best-case scenario for QuickScreen. 

Final Answer: \(\boxed{C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rceil - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}}\)
