","To calculate the activation energy \( E_a \), we use the Arrhenius equation:

\[
k = A e^{-E_a / RT}
\]

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:

\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

For two different temperatures \( T_1 \) and \( T_2 \), we have:

\[
\ln k_f1 = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_1}
\]

\[
\ln k_f2 = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]

Subtracting the first equation from the second:

\[
\ln k_f2 - \ln k_f1 = \frac{E_a}{R} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]

Solving for \( E_a \):

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln k_f2 - \ln k_f1}{\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}} \cdot R
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
E_a = \frac{\ln (10^4) - \ln (10^{-1})}{\frac{1}{272.2 \, \text{K}} - \frac{1}{333.3 \, \text{K}}} \cdot 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{10 - (-1)}{\frac{1}{272.2} - \frac{1}{333.3}} \cdot 2
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{11}{0.0036 - 0.0029} \cdot 2
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{11}{0.0007} \cdot 2
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{22}{0.0007}
\]

\[
E_a = 22000 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy is \(\boxed{22000 \, \text{cal mol}^{-1}}\).
