A proton with mass \(1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}\) and charge \(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) accelerates from rest in a uniform electric field of strength \(500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","(a) The proton is subject to a force \(F = qE\), where \(q = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C}\) and \(E = 500 \, \text{N/C}\).  
\[
F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]
The acceleration is then \(a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2\).  

(b) The proton starts from rest, so the initial speed \(u = 0 \, \text{m/s}\).  The final speed is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\).  The time \(t\) is given by:  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 6.35 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{s} = 6.35 \, \mu\text{s}
\]

**Final Answers**:  
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.76 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  
(b) The time required is \(\boxed{6.35 \, \mu\text{s}}\).
