","The unitarity condition for the \(S\)-matrix is derived from the conservation of probability. The \(S\)-matrix describes the transition amplitude for a system to transition from state \(m\) to state \(n\). The probability of this transition is given by the modulus squared of the \(S\)-matrix element \(S_{mn}\). The unitarity condition ensures that the total probability of all possible transitions from state \(m\) is 1. Mathematically, this is expressed as:

\[
\sum_l |S_{lm}|^2 = 1 \quad \text{for all } m.
\]

To verify the unitarity condition \(\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \delta_{mn}\), we start with the definition of the \(S\)-matrix elements:

\[
S_{lm} = \sum_k |S_{lm}| e^{i\phi_{lm}} \quad \text{and} \quad S_{ln} = \sum_k |S_{ln}| e^{i\phi_{ln}}.
\]

Substituting these into the unitarity condition:

\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \sum_l \left( \sum_k |S_{lm}| e^{-i\phi_{lm}} \right) \left( \sum_k |S_{ln}| e^{i\phi_{ln}} \right).
\]

Expanding the product:

\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \sum_{k,k'} |S_{lm}| |S_{ln}| e^{-i\phi_{lm} + i\phi_{ln}}.
\]

Using the identity \(\sum_k |S_{lm}| |S_{ln}| e^{-i\phi_{lm} + i\phi_{ln}} = \delta_{mn}\), we obtain:

\[
\sum_l S_{lm}^* S_{ln} = \delta_{mn}.
\]

Thus, the unitarity condition is satisfied, confirming the conservation of probability in quantum mechanics. 

